019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,2311,448,220,20,15,6816,48826,220,16,1526,220,20,15,702,1181,6816,312,4082,291,304,9931,11,504,220,20,15,311,220,16,13,1752,1246,1657,6816,653,2176,7289,315,2150,5109,4332,279,1852,6174,15723,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA book with 50 pages numbered 1 through 50 has its pages renumbered in reverse, from 50 to 1. For how many pages do both sets of page numbers share the same ones digit?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"85911905485e90de7a2e55d60a6a0eeea777b69fe81623cab7eb4fb2fbd79cff"},"seconds":30.86997389793396,"seed":817875207355743586,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to determine how many pages have the same ones digit both in their original numbering and their reversed numbering.\n\n1. **Original and Reversed Page Numbers:**\n   - The original page numbers are from 1 to 50.\n   - The reversed page numbers are from 50 to 1.\n\n2. **Identify Matching Ones Digits:**\n   - We need to find pairs of numbers (original, reversed) such that their ones digits match.\n   - The ones digit of a number \\( n \\) in the range 1 to 50 is \\( n \\mod 10 \\).\n   - The ones digit of its reversed counterpart \\( 51 - n \\) is \\( (51 - n) \\mod 10 \\).\n\n3. **Matching Condition:**\n   - For the ones digits to match, we need:\n     \\[\n     n \\mod 10 = (51 - n) \\mod 10\n     \\]\n   - Simplify the condition:\n     \\[\n     n \\mod 10 = 51 - n \\mod 10\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     n \\mod 10 = 1 - n \\mod 10\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2(n \\mod 10) = 1\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     n \\mod 10 = \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n   - Since \\( n \\mod 10 \\) must be an integer, the only possible solution is when \\( n \\mod 10 = 5 \\).\n\n4. **Pages with Ones Digit 5:**\n   - The pages in the original numbering with a ones digit of 5 are: 5, 15, 25, 35, 45.\n   - For each of these pages, the reversed number will also have a ones digit of 5 (since 50 - 5 = 45, 50 - 15 = 35, etc.).\n\n5. **Count the Pages:**\n   - There are 5 pages that satisfy this condition: 5, 15, 25, 35, and 45.\n\nTherefore, the number of pages that have the same ones digit in both the original and reversed numbering is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{5}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
